{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"So I would have been happy to have arrived in the venerable Imperial City of Cologne.... In Coblenz, where my last letter ends, I spent the afternoon and the following morning mostly resting at home — the Consistory Councillor Lange, to whom Schulze had addressed me, was not present, — and in indecision about the weather I let the express coaches, steam packets, and other opportunities pass by; but on Wednesday afternoon it cleared up; I took a boat and went to Neuwied on the beautiful Rhine; I saw the Moravian Brethren' house; — it became too dark to go into the Prince's Brazilian collection. The most beautiful thing was the evening — beautiful moonlight shone over the Rhine, which flowed past my windows; owls, which I had never heard speak in my life, made music in it, — in the morning after 8 o'clock onto the steam packet, an improved market boat. — At first one could be somewhat on the deck, but then it became windy, cold, rainy, finally continuous, heavy, cold rain. The company was thus confined in the cabin, and there were also students in it, who were making their Rhine journey, so with the knapsack, covered with green oilcloth and with a boot sticking out on each side of it, wide new straps, — everything in order, also white, or rather yellow straw hats. So I, although with somewhat more ballast than they, also made my Rhine journey, but therefore saw less, and was inferior to them in that I could not gain the proud consciousness of making a Rhine journey. Already the rainy weather in Coblenz, — above all this Rhine journeying, had spoiled my travel, and if it had not been so far home to you, above all if I had not been afraid of the post coaches, I would have quickly arrived at your place. I travel in general only out of duty and obligation, and would have a hundred times more satisfaction and pleasure if I could divide my time between my studies and you. If you ever come with me to this Rhine, I will lead you differently; on the water one sees neither the Rhine nor the country, — the former not, for one does not see it flowing through the fields and hills, one does not have it as a part of the picture before one, which is its truly beautiful position or flow — nor the country, for one sees only the banks, their boundaries and notices at most that it might be beautiful behind them. — In Linz we went ashore, where I saw the picture recommended by my friend Privy Councillor Schulze, and that in a high-situated church, from where one overlooks the Rhine and the beautiful country. — In abominable rain we went ashore in Bonn. Here I looked for Windischmann and his son-in-law Walther), whom you know from Thibaut, — with the former, who through union in prayer with Prince Hohenlohe?) has been healed for a year of a six-year eye ailment of the kind like Jacobi's, only much worse — and is now perfectly healthy, I got along very well, and we, for the time being, were very satisfied with each other. Walther also pleased me, who remembers you with very heartfelt remembrance, and sends you friendly greetings. — This encounter refreshed me again. To this was added the clearing up of the weather, and so I set out yesterday afternoon in a better disposition.... Bonn is hawkerish, very narrow-streeted, but the surroundings, view, botanical garden — beautiful, very beautiful, but I am still rather in Berlin."}}],"created":1795080223,"model":"qwen","system_fingerprint":"b11429-d81235049","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":762,"prompt_tokens":951,"total_tokens":1713,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-URzjnaMFQGiHfXParzXFeGQcYe4UCClD","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":951,"prompt_ms":967.071,"prompt_per_token_ms":1.0168990536277602,"prompt_per_second":983.3817785870943,"predicted_n":762,"predicted_ms":18132.79,"predicted_per_token_ms":23.827582128777927,"predicted_per_second":41.96816926683648}}